1
00:00:05,505 --> 00:00:06,140
Hello.

2
00:00:06,141 --> 00:00:07,500
I'm Sangsoo Jeong.

3
00:00:07,501 --> 00:00:13,580
This time, I'd like to talk about what I consider an efficient working process.

4
00:00:13,581 --> 00:00:16,620
How do you all start a picture?

5
00:00:16,621 --> 00:00:19,540
Like a lot of people, I start with line drawing.

6
00:00:19,625 --> 00:00:22,741
Some of you probably start with the so-called "avoid line art" approach, painting shapes directly.

7
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:27,141
As I said in Chapter 1, part 1, line drawing is important.

8
00:00:27,780 --> 00:00:34,300
I firmly believe that just because you're aiming for a realistic or semi-realistic style, you should never neglect line drawing.

9
00:00:35,375 --> 00:00:51,080
Even painters who do traditional painting, like oil or watercolor, mostly start a picture with line drawing. Just because the sketch doesn't show in the finished piece doesn't mean you should skip it and go straight to painting shapes because it's faster.

10
00:00:51,081 --> 00:00:55,880
Here, line drawing doesn't mean cleanly inking the lines.

11
00:00:55,960 --> 00:01:02,600
I like to do a brief sketch like this, then draw the picture. I recommend this method to you.

12
00:01:03,490 --> 00:01:06,980
Line drawing acts as a navigator for the picture.

13
00:01:06,980 --> 00:01:14,020
When you set a navigator and drive, line drawing lets you make fewer mistakes and draw something that actually looks like a real picture.

14
00:01:14,021 --> 00:01:18,740
And if you go down the wrong path, you can draw a new line to correct your direction.

15
00:01:18,740 --> 00:01:23,700
I usually do a brief sketch focused on form and silhouette.

16
00:01:23,701 --> 00:01:26,980
Then I lay down a base color and try out the color.

17
00:01:26,981 --> 00:01:30,369
When you've split things into layers, it's fun to test different colors.

18
00:01:31,144 --> 00:01:36,180
After that, along with an overall light setup, I start roughly describing the form.

19
00:01:36,184 --> 00:01:40,100
Once it's progressed a certain amount, I reduce the layers to a minimum and get into the rendering.

20
00:01:40,609 --> 00:01:48,580
This is my basic first-pass working process, and I repeat it whenever I add things like weapons, outfits, or accessories.

21
00:01:50,839 --> 00:01:55,530
At this first-pass stage, without the detail, you get the sense of volume and the overall mood.

22
00:01:55,929 --> 00:02:01,130
If you shrink it down at this point, it doesn't look much different from the finished piece.

23
00:02:01,131 --> 00:02:04,890
The difference in final quality is decided right here.

24
00:02:04,970 --> 00:02:12,570
The reason this is efficient is that you can quickly nail the silhouette and mood without worrying about fine details.

25
00:02:13,164 --> 00:02:18,010
If you do this first pass well, adding detail later feels less burdensome.

26
00:02:18,011 --> 00:02:19,370
Because the foundation is solid.

27
00:02:19,924 --> 00:02:23,671
And it's easy to fix if the detail you've stacked up doesn't sit right.

28
00:02:24,790 --> 00:02:31,400
Imagine sketching and working on every detail all at once: accessories, patterns, weapons, everything.

29
00:02:31,401 --> 00:02:34,840
It's hard to get the overall volume, and hard to fix things.

30
00:02:34,841 --> 00:02:41,480
The design, which you should keep flexibly adjusting and refining as you work, gets trapped inside the sketch.

31
00:02:41,481 --> 00:02:48,280
If the work is commission-based, whether company work, freelance, or commissions, this becomes an even bigger problem.

32
00:02:48,281 --> 00:02:52,600
Because you often have to adjust the picture to fit the client's design.

33
00:02:53,260 --> 00:03:00,540
If you do a solid base first pass, you can branch out in many directions, so the burden is much lighter.

34
00:03:00,541 --> 00:03:05,580
In concept art, a first pass that lets you branch in many directions has lots of benefits.

35
00:03:05,581 --> 00:03:08,780
You can quickly show a design mood with the first pass alone.

36
00:03:09,559 --> 00:03:13,340
You can try out lots of design options, like playing from selection.

37
00:03:13,740 --> 00:03:16,940
If the job needs variations, it also cuts down your working time.

38
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:21,381
Design with accurate form and volume communicates well.

39
00:03:21,960 --> 00:03:25,240
So how much should you sketch?

40
00:03:25,241 --> 00:03:29,320
I'll cover that in a later chapter, but let me touch on it briefly.

41
00:03:29,321 --> 00:03:31,720
I usually do a very simple sketch.

42
00:03:32,230 --> 00:03:35,361
The minimum sketch needed to support the form.

43
00:03:35,670 --> 00:03:38,600
Sometimes I don't even draw the eyes, nose, or mouth.

44
00:03:38,601 --> 00:03:45,320
But that's only to save time. I recommend sketching a bit more precisely, even if it's brief.

45
00:03:45,321 --> 00:03:48,600
I'm not talking about detail in the sketch itself.

46
00:03:48,601 --> 00:03:52,700
In my process, detail is added in the later stages.

47
00:03:52,701 --> 00:03:57,980
The sketch focuses on accurate form and silhouette rather than detail.

48
00:03:57,981 --> 00:04:00,620
Start big, finish detailed.

49
00:04:00,621 --> 00:04:04,060
That's a statement that runs through the whole process.

50
00:04:04,061 --> 00:04:08,220
Rendering body muscles, you also start from the big and move to the small.

51
00:04:08,221 --> 00:04:11,740
Start with a big brush and finish with a small brush.

52
00:04:11,820 --> 00:04:15,420
You decide the big framework of the design and then think about fine detail.

53
00:04:16,020 --> 00:04:19,621
Catch the big flow first, then draw the fine bits.

54
00:04:19,990 --> 00:04:21,480
Always remember.

55
00:04:21,481 --> 00:04:23,880
Start big, finish detailed.

56
00:04:23,960 --> 00:04:26,721
That will make your work efficient.

57
00:04:27,400 --> 00:04:32,040
This time, we talked about an efficient working process.

58
00:04:32,041 --> 00:04:38,520
Next time, before we get into the real work, we'll look at setting up your working environment and the brushes I use.
